WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.020
Build, summarise and check your tracking sheet in Google Sheets.

2
00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:08.640
This is the video for module 2 of Reporting and Performance Tracking.

3
00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:24.260
Google Sheets is a free spreadsheet app that works on your phone. The golden rule is one row per record. Every row is one event, like one sale. Every column holds one kind of information, like the date or the item.

4
00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:31.310
Use dropdown lists, so WhatsApp is always spelled the same way, and keep totals on a separate Summary tab.

5
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:44.850
Avoid empty rows, merged cells and totals typed in the middle of your data. And type numbers only, without the naira sign. These simple habits are what let formulas and charts work later.

6
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:53.060
Record each sale as soon as it happens. Entering a whole week from memory on Sunday night leads to missing sales.

7
00:00:54.310 --> 00:01:06.850
Bisi types the quantity and the price, and a formula works out each amount: 7,500, 6,000 and 3,000 naira. Her Paid column is a Yes or No dropdown.

8
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:20.130
She sold 16,500 naira that day. 13,500 is paid, and 3,000 is still owed. Now she can easily count unpaid orders and follow up.

9
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:27.330
Formulas always start with an equals sign, and recalculate by themselves when you add data.

10
00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:34.980
SUM adds up your sales. AVERAGE finds the average sale. COUNTA counts how many rows you have.

11
00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:44.320
COUNTIF counts matching cells, like orders marked No for unpaid. SUMIF adds only matching rows, like WhatsApp sales.

12
00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:50.480
And percentage change is the new figure minus the old, divided by the old.

13
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:56.720
At the end of March, Bisi's SUM shows 280,000 naira in sales.

14
00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:06.690
Her SUMIF splits it by channel: 142,000 on WhatsApp, 98,000 walk-in and 40,000 on Instagram.

15
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:12.400
These add up to 280,000, which confirms nothing is missing.

16
00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:24.280
February was 250,000. 280,000 minus 250,000, divided by 250,000, is 12%.

17
00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:30.990
Her target was 300,000, so she reached 93.3% of target.

18
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.400
And COUNTIF shows 5 orders still unpaid. Those are debts to chase.

19
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:47.670
When a number is already a percentage, be careful. If conversion rises from 20% to 25%, subtracting gives 5 percentage points.

20
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:55.220
Dividing 5 by 20 gives a 25% increase. Both are correct, but very different.

21
00:02:55.570 --> 00:03:04.120
The safe habit is to write: up 5 percentage points, from 20% to 25%. Then the reader sees the real change.

22
00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:15.680
One duplicated row, or a price typed with an extra zero, can turn a weak month into a great one on paper. So check every week. First, turn on a filter.

23
00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:23.330
Sort the amount column from largest to smallest. Is 25000 really 2500?

24
00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.180
Filter the unpaid orders and follow them up.

25
00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:32.780
Compare the week's paid total with your bank, Opay or Moniepoint history.

26
00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:40.520
Fix any errors, and write what you changed on a Notes tab with the date. Never invent numbers to fill a gap.

27
00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:45.410
Reconciling means checking your sheet against your money records.

28
00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:54.050
Her app shows 68,500 naira coming in. Her sheet shows only 63,500.

29
00:03:55.300 --> 00:04:02.550
The difference is 5,000 naira. She finds a phone order that was never entered, adds it, and notes the correction.

30
00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:11.970
If personal money goes into the same account, keep a separate business account, or mark personal transfers clearly so they are not counted as sales.

31
00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:28.210
To remember. One row per event. Use dropdowns, and type numbers without the naira sign. Let formulas do the counting. Be clear about percentage points. And check your data every week against your bank app, noting every correction.

32
00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:43.440
Now practise. Build your Data tab with clear headings, dropdowns and five real records. Add a Summary tab with totals and progress against your target. Then run your first data check and start your Notes tab.

33
00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:45.730
Continue with the lessons below.
